IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `je54-nouns`

-- je54-schedule-definitions: Article 4 and the Schedule (Part 1, the defined expressions; Part 2, the index) of the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, for a given TEXT of the Law (the caller passes the text in force on the
-- selecting date; je54-seat composes it with the vintage table).  Copyable: it imports only prelude, daydate and the nouns.
--
-- The Schedule says either "shall mean" / "means" (the meaning is complete: a thing outside it is outside the word) or "includes" / "shall include" / "shall be construed as including" (the meaning is added to:
-- a thing the Schedule does not name may still be within the word).  Magistrate does both.  The form is data (`Form of a definition`) and the difference is tested.
-- Part 1 is the same in all seven deposited texts except that "child" and "parent" were added from 24 November 2025 (V2e): the seven Part 1 blocks were compared with a hash on 2026-10-28 (NOTES.md section 5).
-- Before 1 January 2019 only "month" and "midnight" are answered, from 28 March 2003, by inference from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation (NOTES.md section 5); every other word refuses.
-- Selecting date: the day the question is asked about.

§ `Article 4: Definitions`

-- Refusal (Article 4(1)): what the Schedule functions say when a contrary intention appears.
-- src:21 | (1) The definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule shall, unless the contrary intention appears, apply to every enactment, whenever passed or made.
-- src:22 | (2) Part 2 of the Schedule shall have effect only as an index of definitions in other enactments which are of extended application.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 4 (src:21-22)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 4(1): a contrary intention appears, and the Schedule does not decide` MEANS
    REFUSE "a contrary intention appears: Article 4(1) says the definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule apply unless the contrary intention appears, and does not say what the word then means"

-- Refusal (the Schedule): what the Schedule functions say for a day before 1 January 2019, except "month" and "midnight".
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:1 | Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:2 | A LAW for promoting brevity and uniformity in enactments
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:3 | Commencement [see endnotes]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954 as consolidated for 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:1-3)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Schedule: no text of the Law before the first consolidated version` MEANS
    REFUSE "before the first consolidated version (1 January 2019)"

-- Refusal (the Schedule): what the Schedule functions say for a day before 28 March 2003, before which not even "month" and "midnight" are answered.
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:1 | Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:2 | A LAW for promoting brevity and uniformity in enactments
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:3 | Commencement [see endnotes]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954 as consolidated for 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:1-3)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Schedule: no text of the Law before 28 March 2003` MEANS
    REFUSE "before the first consolidated version (1 January 2019); and before 28 March 2003 the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation do not date the Schedule definitions of month and midnight either (L.16/2003 inserted the Schedule on that day)"

-- Refusal (the Schedule): the word is not defined in Part 1 of the text in force on the day.
-- src:80 | Schedule[19]
-- src:81 | (Article 4)
-- src:82 | PART 1[20]
-- src:83 | (Article 4(1))
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1 (src:80-83)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the Schedule does not define this word in the text in force on that day` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Schedule does not define this word in the text of the Law in force on that day"

-- Refusal (Article 4(2)): Part 2 is only an index and never supplies a meaning.
-- src:122 | PART 2
-- src:123 | (Article 4(2))
-- src:124 | Index of definitions in other enactments
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 2 and Article 4(2) (src:22), (src:122-124)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Part 2 of the Schedule is only an index of definitions in other enactments` MEANS
    REFUSE "Part 2 of the Schedule has effect only as an index of definitions in other enactments which are of extended application (Article 4(2)); it never supplies a meaning"

-- Refusal (the Schedule, "month"): neither the Law nor the Schedule says how a calendar month is added to a date.
-- src:105 | “month” shall mean a calendar month;
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1, definition of “month” (src:105)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`neither the Law nor the Schedule says how a calendar month is added to a date` MEANS
    REFUSE "the Schedule defines a month as a calendar month and the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954 does not say how a calendar month is added to a date, or what happens at the end of a month"

§ `The Schedule, Part 1: the defined expressions`

-- The definitions follow, one per line, quoted from the lead-in (src:85) on.  The ruler for the positional constructions below is: term, form, the condition it states, the things it names, the Schedule's words.
-- src:85 | The definitions referred to in Article 4(1) are –
-- term, form, the condition it states, the things it names, the Schedule's own words (every string was checked to be a substring of the source line quoted above it)
-- Article 4(1), Schedule Part 1: what the Schedule says the word “act” includes, as data (the definition, its form, its condition if any, the things it names).
-- src:86 | “act” includes omission;
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1, definition of “act” (src:86)
`the definition in the Schedule of act` MEANS
    `Defined expression` OF "act", `includes`, NOTHING,
        (LIST "omission"),
        "includes omission"

-- Article 4(1), Schedule Part 1: what the Schedule says the word “affidavit” includes, as data (the definition, its form, its condition if any, the things it names).
-- src:87 | “affidavit” shall, in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to affirm or declare instead of swearing, include affirmation and declaration;
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1, definition of “affidavit” (src:87)
`the definition in the Schedule of affidavit` MEANS
    `Defined expression` OF "affidavit", `includes`, JUST "in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to affirm or declare instead of swearing",
        (LIST "affirmation", "declaration"),
        "shall, in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to affirm or declare instead of swearing, include affirmation and declaration"

