§ `Looking a word up`

-- Article 4(1): the list of definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule in a given text of the Law.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1 (src:85-121)
GIVEN v IS A `Text of the Law`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Defined expression`
`the definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule in the text` v MEANS
    CONSIDER v
    WHEN `V2e, 24 November 2025 to 18 June 2026` THEN `the definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule in the texts V2e and V2f`
    WHEN `V2f, 19 June 2026 to current` THEN `the definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule in the texts V2e and V2f`
    OTHERWISE `the definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule in the texts V1 to V2d`

-- Article 4(1): look up a word among a list of definitions, with the basis on which the list is read.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1 (src:85-121)
GIVEN `the word` IS A STRING
      list IS A LIST OF `Defined expression`
      basis IS A `Basis of a reading`
GIVETH A `Definition with its basis`
`the definition of the word` `the word` `found among` list `on the basis` basis MEANS
    CONSIDER filter (GIVEN d YIELD d's term EQUALS `the word`) list
    WHEN found FOLLOWED BY more THEN `Definition with its basis` WITH definition IS found, `the basis` IS basis
    WHEN EMPTY THEN `the Schedule does not define this word in the text in force on that day`

-- The definition of a word in Part 1 of the Schedule, in the text of the Law given.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1 (src:85-121)
GIVEN `the word` IS A STRING
      v IS A `Text of the Law`
GIVETH A `Definition with its basis`
`the Schedule's definition of` `the word` `in the text` v MEANS
    CONSIDER v
    WHEN `no text before 28 March 2003` THEN
        IF `the word` EQUALS "month" OR `the word` EQUALS "midnight"
        THEN `the Schedule: no text of the Law before 28 March 2003`
        ELSE `the Schedule: no text of the Law before the first consolidated version`
    WHEN `the inferred text from 28 March 2003 to 31 December 2018` THEN
        IF `the word` EQUALS "month" OR `the word` EQUALS "midnight"
        THEN `the definition of the word` `the word` `found among` (`the definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule in the text` v) `on the basis` `inferred from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation, not verified against L.16/2003`
        ELSE `the Schedule: no text of the Law before the first consolidated version`
    OTHERWISE `the definition of the word` `the word` `found among` (`the definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule in the text` v) `on the basis` `read from the deposited text in force on that day`

§ `What a definition does for a thing`

-- The Schedule's words decide.  A thing the definition names is within the word.  A thing it does not name: for "means" it is outside; for "includes" it is not shut out and the word's own meaning decides; for a
-- definition that does both (Magistrate) fork I6 decides (taken: the named things are all that the word covers).  A definition that states a condition ("in relation to a day", "in the case of a person
-- allowed to affirm") applies only where the condition is met.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1 (src:85-121)
GIVEN d IS A `Defined expression`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the definition states a condition` d MEANS
    CONSIDER d's `the condition it states`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST c THEN TRUE

-- Article 4(1): what a definition does for a thing asked about: within the word, outside it, left to the word's own meaning, or not applying because its condition is not met.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1 (src:85-121)
GIVEN d IS A `Defined expression`
      named IS A BOOLEAN
      `the condition is met` IS A BOOLEAN
      reading IS A `Reading of a definition that both means and includes` TYPICALLY `the named things are all that the word covers`
GIVETH A `Reach of a defined expression`
`what the definition does for a thing` d named `the condition is met` reading MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the definition states a condition` d AND NOT `the condition is met` THEN `the definition does not apply: the condition it states is not met`
           IF named THEN `within the word, as the Schedule says`
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER d's form
               WHEN `means` THEN `outside the word, because the Schedule says means`
               WHEN `includes` THEN `not named by the Schedule, which says includes and so does not shut it out: the word's own meaning decides`
               WHEN `means and includes` THEN
                   CONSIDER reading
                   WHEN `the named things are all that the word covers` THEN `outside the word, because the Schedule says means`
                   WHEN `the named things do not exhaust the word` THEN `not named by the Schedule, which says includes and so does not shut it out: the word's own meaning decides`

-- Article 4(1): the definitions apply, unless the contrary intention appears, to every enactment, whenever passed or made.  The day the enactment was passed or made is not an input: it does not matter.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 4(1) (src:21)
GIVEN `the word` IS A STRING
      v IS A `Text of the Law`
      named IS A BOOLEAN
      `the condition is met` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
      reading IS A `Reading of a definition that both means and includes` TYPICALLY `the named things are all that the word covers`
GIVETH A `Reach of a defined expression`
`Article 4(1): how a word defined in Part 1 of the Schedule reaches a thing` `the word` v named `the condition is met` `a contrary intention appears` reading MEANS
    LET found MEANS `the Schedule's definition of` `the word` `in the text` v IN
        IF `a contrary intention appears`
        THEN `Article 4(1): a contrary intention appears, and the Schedule does not decide`
        ELSE `what the definition does for a thing` (found's definition) named `the condition is met` reading

§ `Part 2: an index only (Article 4(2))`

-- Part 2 has a heading and no entries in any of the seven texts.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 4(2), Schedule, Part 2 (src:22), (src:122-124)
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the entries of the index in Part 2 of the Schedule` MEANS EMPTY

-- Article 4(2): the meaning of a word according to Part 2 of the Schedule: always a refusal, because Part 2 is only an index.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 4(2), Schedule, Part 2 (src:22), (src:122-124)
GIVEN `the word` IS A STRING
GIVETH A `Defined expression`
`Article 4(2): the meaning of a word according to Part 2 of the Schedule` `the word` MEANS
    `Part 2 of the Schedule is only an index of definitions in other enactments`

§ `Midnight, and a calendar month`

-- "midnight", in relation to a day, shall mean the last moment of that day.  A thing done by midnight on D was done on D or earlier: its calendar day, read in the time zone given, is not after D; an instant
-- on the following day at 00:00:00 is after the midnight of D.  The time zone is not named by the Law: the `Local moment` is the moment on a clock in the zone in which days are read (NOTES.md section 7).
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1, definition of “midnight” (src:103)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      m IS A `Local moment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the moment is not after the midnight of` d `being` m MEANS
    m's `calendar day` AT MOST d

-- Schedule, "midnight": whether a thing done at a moment was done by midnight on a day, midnight in relation to a day being the last moment of that day.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1, definition of “midnight” (src:103)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      m IS A `Local moment`
      v IS A `Text of the Law`
GIVETH A `Yes or no with its basis`
`the thing was done by midnight on` d `being` m `in the text` v MEANS
    CONSIDER v
    WHEN `no text before 28 March 2003` THEN `the Schedule: no text of the Law before 28 March 2003`
    WHEN `the inferred text from 28 March 2003 to 31 December 2018` THEN
        `Yes or no with its basis` WITH `the answer` IS `the moment is not after the midnight of` d `being` m, `the basis` IS `inferred from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation, not verified against L.16/2003`
    OTHERWISE
        `Yes or no with its basis` WITH `the answer` IS `the moment is not after the midnight of` d `being` m, `the basis` IS `read from the deposited text in force on that day`

-- Schedule, "month": the day one calendar month after a day: always a refusal, because neither the Law nor the Schedule says how a month is added at a month end.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1, definition of “month” (src:105)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day one calendar month after` d MEANS
    `neither the Law nor the Schedule says how a calendar month is added to a date`
